The warning message means that the background configuration files for the Tor service were updated or modified on your hard drive (often due to an package update or direct manual changes) since the last time the initialization system loaded them.
Because of this, systemd is alerting you that it is still running Tor using its old memory profile instead of your new on-disk configurations.
To clear the warning and ensure Tor runs perfectly with the updated files, run these two commands in sequence:
## 🛠️ 1. Reload the Systemd Daemon
This forces Ubuntu to read all background configuration files fresh off the disk and update its service registry:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload

## 🚀 2. Restart the Tor Service
Now that the new configurations are loaded into system memory, restart the Tor service to cleanly apply them and establish fresh routing paths:

sudo systemctl restart tor

## 🔍 3. Verify it Cleared
You can verify that the configuration loaded cleanly without warnings by checking its active runtime profile:

sudo systemctl status tor

Look for Active: active (running) in the text stream, which should print without any trailing systemd reload warnings.
------------------------------
## 🌐 Quick Check: Is Your Tor Routing Fully Online?
Once the service restarts cleanly, verify that your terminal can route traffic successfully through the local proxy link on port 9050:

curl --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:9050 https://torproject.org

If working, this will return a JSON block confirming your active Tor exit node IP address.
Did the daemon-reload command successfully clear the warning message when you checked the service status?

The status active (exited) with ExecStart=/bin/true means the main master Tor service ran successfully, but it did not actually launch a Tor proxy instance.
On modern Ubuntu systems, the tor.service file acts purely as a "master wrapper" manager. The actual background proxy process runs inside a separate, dynamically managed instance called tor@default.service.
To turn on the actual Tor proxy connection, run these commands to activate the specific default instance:
## 🛠️ Step 1: Start and Enable the Default Tor Instance
Run these commands to tell systemd to start the actual network proxy daemon:

# Start the real default Tor process
sudo systemctl start tor@default
# Enable it so it launches automatically on system boot
sudo systemctl enable tor@default

## 🔍 Step 2: Verify the Real Proxy Status
Now, check the status of the specific proxy instance you just launched:

sudo systemctl status tor@default

Look for Active: active (running) in green text. Unlike the master wrapper, this one should stay actively running in the background and show a real process ID (PID).
------------------------------
## 🌐 Step 3: Test the Connection Proxy Port
Once tor@default is running, confirm that it is actively listening on its standard local port (9050) to handle your python script requests: [1] 

ss -nltp | grep tor

You should see a line matching 127.0.0.1:9050 or *:9050 indicating the socket interface is completely open.
Did the status change to active (running) when you checked the tor@default service endpoint?

